{"id": "309617af-e8bb-48a4-880c-382f9729e79f", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll audit your EBS setup in us-east-1 for production readiness. That covers volume types, encryption, snapshots and backups, attachment state, performance headroom, and cost or hygiene issues. I'll report back with a summary.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_ZE4uTe3biIzrPPr4DK08bm", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "ebs-prod-readiness-review", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["exploring-metrics", "discovering-topology"], "prompt": "{
  "task": "Perform a READ-ONLY production-readiness review of Amazon EBS in us-east-1 and return a concise, prioritized findings report. Do not make any mutating calls.",
  "context": "The user has a production launch next week (today is 2026-11-11) and wants to know if their EBS storage is production-ready. Start with the agent space account 012345678901_1, and also check any other AWS accounts returned by list_associations that are monitor/source accounts, in us-east-1. Group findings by account.",
  "resources": ["All EBS volumes, snapshots owned by the account(s), and EC2 instances they attach to in us-east-1", "Data Lifecycle Manager policies", "AWS Backup plans/selections covering EBS", "EBS default encryption setting and default KMS key", "EBS snapshot public-sharing block setting"],
  "timeframe": "Metrics: last 14 days up to now (2026-11-11T19:13:00Z)",
  "criteria": "Assess and report on: (1) Encryption: unencrypted volumes/snapshots, whether account-level default EBS encryption is enabled, KMS key usage (default aws/ebs vs CMK). (2) Backup/DR: volumes with no recent snapshot or no DLM/AWS Backup coverage, snapshot age/recency, snapshots shared publicly or cross-account, whether cross-region copies exist for critical volumes. (3) Volume types and sizing: gp2 volumes that should be migrated to gp3, io1/io2 usage, provisioned IOPS/throughput vs actual use, st1/sc1 use for boot or latency-sensitive workloads, Multi-Attach usage. (4) Performance headroom: from CloudWatch AWS/EBS metrics (VolumeQueueLength, VolumeReadOps/WriteOps, VolumeThroughputPercentage, BurstBalance for gp2/st1/sc1, VolumeIdleTime) and EC2 instance-level EBS bandwidth/IOPS limits flag volumes near limits or with exhausted burst balance. (5) Health/state: volumes in error/impaired status, volumes with impaired status checks (describe_volume_status), unattached (available) volumes, volumes not set to DeleteOnTermination appropriately for data volumes, stale snapshots/orphaned AMI snapshots. (6) Availability: single-AZ distribution of volumes for the same workload, tags missing (Name/Environment/Owner) hindering ownership. (7) Any recent EBS-related CloudWatch alarms or missing alarms on critical volumes. Return: counts and totals first (volumes, size GiB, by type, by encryption state), then a prioritized list of issues (Critical / Should-fix before launch / Nice-to-have) with specific volume/snapshot IDs (cap at ~10 IDs per issue, then give counts), and an overall readiness verdict. Keep output compact. Note anything you could not check due to permissions."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Audit EBS volumes, snapshots, backups in us-east-1"}], "created_at": null}

